@meta
id: 16-05
title: The file you just left
chapter: 16
type: lesson
skills: buffer-switch
par: 125
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-Tab, which cycles through a popup you have to watch
open: order.entity.ts order.repository.ts

@brief
Most switching is between two files. Entity and repository. Test and
code. Provider and the service it calls. Vim keeps track of the buffer
you were in before this one and calls it the alternate file; `Ctrl-^`
swaps you to it, and pressing it again swaps you back. No name, no
number, one chord.

In `:ls` the alternate buffer carries a `#` flag. Until you have
switched at least once there is no alternate and `Ctrl-^` says so with
E23, which is why the first hop here is `:bn`.

The ticket: orders need a `placedAt` timestamp, and `OrderStatus` gains
a `REFUNDED` value.

  1. `order.entity.ts`: `placedAt: Date;` directly under `customerId`.
  2. `order.repository.ts`: `placedAt: record.placedAt,` directly under
     the `customerId` line in `toEntity`.
  3. Back in the entity: append `| 'REFUNDED'` to the `OrderStatus`
     union, before the semicolon.

Write before each hop.

@keys
| `Ctrl-^` | go to the alternate file (the one you were in before) |
| `Ctrl-6` | the same key on keyboards where `^` needs Shift |
| `:bn` | next buffer, for the first hop when there is no alternate yet |
| `#` in `:ls` | which buffer is the alternate |

@hints
Entity first: `/customerId`, `o`, the field, `Esc`, `:w`. `:bn` to the
repository.
--
In the repository `customerId` appears three times; `/record.cu` goes
straight to the one in `toEntity`. Add the mapping, `:w`.
--
`Ctrl-^` takes you back to the entity. `/SHIPPED`, `$` to the
semicolon, `i`, type ` | 'REFUNDED'`, `Esc`, `:wqa`.

@solution
    /customerId<Enter>  o placedAt: Date; <Esc>  :w<Enter>  :bn<Enter>
    /record.cu<Enter>  o placedAt: record.placedAt, <Esc>  :w<Enter>  Ctrl-^
    /SHIPPED<Enter>  $  i  | 'REFUNDED' <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

About a hundred and ten keystrokes; the third hop cost one of them.

`Ctrl-^` is the key for the way people actually work, which is two
files at a time with occasional excursions. Combined with `:b name` for
the excursions it covers almost every switch you will make in a day.
It also survives the excursion: go to a third file, `:b` back to the
entity, and `Ctrl-^` now bounces between the entity and the third
file, because "alternate" always means "the one before this one".

VS Code's Ctrl-Tab is the same idea with a popup in the way. Here there
is nothing to watch; the buffer is on screen before your finger is off
the key.

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  placedAt: Date;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED' | 'REFUNDED';

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      placedAt: record.placedAt,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}
